course: medicine
lesson: 19
title: Antibiotics, viruses and side effects
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Germs come in different kinds, and the two you hear about most are bacteria and viruses: bacteria are tiny living cells, while viruses are far smaller and can only multiply inside our own cells.
explain: Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria or stop them growing, but they cannot touch a virus, so they do nothing for a cold, flu or most sore throats.
explain: Picture a key that only fits one lock: an antibiotic fits the machinery of bacteria, and a virus simply has no such lock.
explain: Every time antibiotics are used, the few bacteria that happen to survive can multiply, so careless use breeds germs the medicine no longer kills; this is called antibiotic resistance.
explain: All medicines can have side effects, unwanted effects on top of the ones you want, which is why doctors weigh the good against the bad before they prescribe.
fact: Antibiotics kill bacteria or stop them multiplying, and they have no effect on viruses.
fact: Colds, flu and most coughs and sore throats are caused by viruses, so antibiotics do not help them get better faster.
fact: A chest infection caused by bacteria can improve within a couple of days on antibiotics, but doctors ask patients to take the course exactly as prescribed and to check with them before stopping.
fact: Antibiotic resistance means the bacteria change so the medicine stops working; it is the bacteria that become resistant, not the person's body.
fact: Using leftover antibiotics is unsafe, because they may be the wrong medicine or dose for a new illness and they help resistance spread.
fact: Antibiotics also kill some of the helpful bacteria in the gut, so a runny tummy is a common side effect and usually not an allergy.
fact: The leaflet in a medicine box lists every side effect ever reported, and most people get none or only mild ones.
fact: Some older hay fever tablets (antihistamines) reach the brain and cause sleepiness, and pharmacists can suggest non-drowsy kinds.
fact: An itchy rash after a new medicine can be an allergic reaction, so the doctor changes the medicine and writes the reaction in the patient's record.
fact: Some medicines interact, making each other stronger or weaker, so doctors and pharmacists need to know about every medicine and herbal remedy a person takes.
fact: Alexander Fleming noticed in 1928 that a mould killed bacteria on a dish, and Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and their team turned penicillin into a medicine in the 1940s; eating mouldy food is not a medicine and can make you ill.
fact: Antibiotics have saved millions of lives from infections that once often killed, such as infected wounds and pneumonia, an infection of the lungs.
exercise: Why won't antibiotics help a cold? | Colds are caused by viruses, and antibiotics only work on bacteria | Colds are too mild for antibiotics to notice | The cold germ hides in the nose where tablets can't reach | Antibiotics only work in summer
exercise: What do antibiotics do? | Kill bacteria or stop them growing | Kill every kind of germ, viruses too | Boost the immune system to fight anything | Lower a fever by cooling the blood
exercise: You've felt better for a day on antibiotics. What does the doctor usually want? | Keep taking them as prescribed and ask before stopping | Stop at once because the germs are all gone for good now | Halve the dose to save some for later | Swap them for vitamin C tablets
exercise: What is antibiotic resistance? | Bacteria change so the antibiotic no longer kills them | The body gets used to antibiotics and simply ignores them | The tablets go weak if kept too long | The person becomes allergic to all medicines
exercise: Who or what becomes resistant? | The bacteria | The patient's body | The tablet | The patient's blood
exercise: Why do antibiotics often give a runny tummy? | They also kill helpful bacteria in the gut | They are always an allergy for every child who takes them | They are made from soap, which upsets the tummy | They stop the stomach from making any acid at all
exercise: What does a long list of side effects in a leaflet mean? | Every effect ever reported is listed, though most people get none or mild ones | You will get most of them | The medicine is very dangerous | Only adults get side effects
exercise: Why can some hay fever tablets make you sleepy? | Some older antihistamines reach the brain and cause drowsiness | The pollen in your nose makes you tired | The dose is always too strong for children | Sneezing less makes the body bored
exercise: Why is it wrong to take leftover antibiotics from last time? | They may be the wrong type or dose and help resistance spread | They turn into a dangerous poison about a week after you open them | They only work on the illness they were bought for by name | Old tablets are always stronger
exercise: A new medicine made you itchy. What does the doctor do? | Change it and record the reaction | Double the dose so the itch goes | Tell you to ignore it | Ban all medicines for you forever
exercise: Why did Mum ask if the new medicine is safe with the asthma inhaler? | Some medicines interact and change each other's effects | Two medicines always cancel out | Inhalers stop all tablets working | Doctors are not allowed to give anyone two medicines at once
exercise: Who first noticed that a mould killed bacteria, in 1928? | Alexander Fleming | Louis Pasteur | Edward Jenner | Florence Nightingale
exercise: When did penicillin become a medicine people could take? | In the 1940s | In the 1840s | In 1928 straight away | In the 1990s
exercise: What size are viruses compared with bacteria? | Much smaller, and they multiply only inside our cells | Much bigger, so we can see them | Exactly the same size | Bigger, and they live happily on their own outside our cells
exercise: What should you tell a doctor or pharmacist before a new medicine? | Every medicine and herbal remedy you take | Only the medicines that taste really horrible | Nothing, because they can always just guess it | Just your favourite food and your favourite colour
test: mate says "get antibiotics for ur flu, it'll be gone tomorrow". Right? | No, flu is a virus and antibiotics don't touch it | Yes, antibiotics clear flu in a day, everyone knows it | Yes, if you take two at once it goes away even faster | No, flu just needs much stronger antibiotics than a cold
test: Grandad says "my body got used to antibiotics so they stopped working". What's actually happened? | The bacteria became resistant, not his body | His blood has turned against all kinds of tablets | His stomach has learned how to digest them first | He is now allergic to everything a doctor gives
test: What would happen if everyone took antibiotics for every cold? | More bacteria would become resistant and antibiotics would fail more often | Colds would disappear from the world | Nothing, because it does no harm | Viruses would turn into bacteria
test: ugh the leaflet says headache, rash, dizzy, sick... I'm not taking this rubbish. Best reply? | Most people get no side effects or mild ones, and the doctor weighed it up | You will get all of those, so don't take it | Leaflets are only there for fun | Side effects only happen to adults
test: In a cartoon the hero eats mouldy bread and his infection is cured. What's wrong? | Mouldy bread is not a medicine and can make you ill | Nothing, mould is penicillin | He should have eaten mouldy cheese instead, it works better | It only works on toast
test: Ellie got diarrhoea on antibiotics and says she's allergic now. Most likely? | It's a common side effect from gut bacteria, not usually an allergy | She is definitely allergic to all medicines | The antibiotic was fake | She caught a new virus from the tablet
test: Which is true about side effects? | They are unwanted effects on top of the wanted ones | They only happen with fake medicines | They are always serious | They mean the medicine is working twice as hard as usual
test: Mum's got some old antibiotics in a drawer. What's best? | Take them back to the pharmacy for safe disposal | Save them in the drawer for the next sore throat | Give them to the dog if it ever looks a bit poorly | Flush them down the toilet so nobody can find them
test: You're sleepy in maths after your new hay fever tablet. What's the sensible step? | Tell a grown-up, a non-drowsy kind may suit you | Take two more to wake up | Stop eating breakfast | Nothing, it means the tablet is too strong to ever take
test: Why did doctors before the 1940s fear a scratch from a rusty fence more than doctors do now? | Infected wounds could not be treated with antibiotics then | Fences in the past were much sharper and rustier than they are now | People in those days had much thinner skin than people today | Bacteria did not exist yet, so the danger came from the rust
test: Doctor says "this is viral, no antibiotics". Dad gets cross. Who's right? | The doctor, antibiotics don't help viruses and can do harm | Dad, antibiotics help everything | Neither, viruses always need a jab from the nurse every time | Dad, if he asks twice
exam: Identify the principal reason for which antibacterial agents are not prescribed in cases of the common cold. | The causative organism is a virus, upon which such agents exert no effect | The condition resolves too swiftly for any medicament | The agents are reserved exclusively for adults | The agents aggravate nasal congestion
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning antimicrobial resistance. | It is the bacteria, not the patient, that acquire resistance | It is the patient's immune system that becomes resistant | It arises solely from expired medicaments | It is confined to hospitals and cannot occur in any other place
exam: Whereas a patient has been furnished with a course of antibiotics and reports improvement after two days, determine the conduct generally advised. | Continuance as prescribed, with consultation prior to cessation | Immediate cessation and retention of the remainder for future illness | Doubling of the dose to hasten recovery | Substitution with a herbal preparation
exam: Ascertain the individual credited with the observation, in 1928, of the antibacterial action of a mould. | Alexander Fleming | Edward Jenner | Louis Pasteur | Joseph Lister
